#|
Kanata configuration for a Brazilian ABNT2 keyboard on Linux. Ctrl also lives
on Caps Lock and is remapped at the kernel level, so kanata sees it as a
regular left Ctrl and passes it through untouched. Esc and Alt (the i3 mod key)
are not remapped on the base layer, and neither are the letter keys.

AltGr and the left Win key are layer keys. AltGr opens the sym layer
(programming symbols) and Win opens the num layer (a numpad on the right hand
and brackets on the left hand). Tapping either key toggles its layer on for the
whole keyboard, and tapping the same key again, or pressing Esc, returns to the
base layer. Holding either key activates its layer for as long as the key stays
down.

Kanata works with physical key codes, so every symbol below is defined by the
physical key that types it on an ABNT2 layout. The operating system layout
must therefore be br with the abnt2 variant, otherwise the symbols will come
out wrong. Key names in the comments use US names, which describe the physical
position, and a few of those keys print a different character on ABNT2.
|#

(defcfg
  #|
  Also process keys that are not listed in defsrc. Without this, the tap-hold
  keys below would ignore unmapped keys, such as Shift, Space and the digits,
  when deciding between tap and hold.
  |#
  process-unmapped-keys yes
)

#|
The ABNT2 layout has two keys that a US keyboard does not have: the backslash
and vertical bar key to the left of Z, and the slash and question mark key to
the left of the right Shift. They are given custom names here, built from
their Linux evdev key codes, which are 86 (KEY_102ND) and 89 (KEY_RO).
|#
(deflocalkeys-linux
  abntbs    86
  abntslash 89
)

#|
Only Esc, the left Win key and AltGr, plus the three letter rows and the key
right of P, are listed here. Every other key passes through unchanged thanks to
process-unmapped-keys.
|#
(defsrc
  esc  lmet ralt
  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [
  a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    scln
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Timeouts in milliseconds. The first number of a tap-hold action is the
tap-repress timeout (how soon a repeated press right after a tap still counts
as a tap). The second one is the hold timeout. The layer keys use
tap-hold-press, so the hold starts as soon as another key is pressed, and a
press shorter than the hold timeout toggles the layer instead. If a slow tap
still turns into a hold, adjust hold-layer in steps of 20.
|#
(defvar
  tap        200
  hold-layer 300
)

(defalias
  ;; The layer keys. A tap toggles the layer on until it is toggled off, and a
  ;; hold activates the layer for as long as the key stays down.
  numkey (tap-hold-press $tap $hold-layer (layer-switch num) (layer-while-held num))
  symkey (tap-hold-press $tap $hold-layer (layer-switch sym) (layer-while-held sym))

  ;; Both of these return to the base layer, and escback also types an Esc.
  back    (layer-switch base)
  escback (multi esc (layer-switch base))

  ;; From here on, every alias is named after the character it types on an
  ;; ABNT2 layout. The first ones are shifted characters from the digit row.
  ex   S-1       ;; !
  at   S-2       ;; @
  hsh  S-3       ;; hash sign
  dol  S-4       ;; $
  pct  S-5       ;; %
  amp  S-7       ;; &
  ast  S-8       ;; *
  lpar S-9       ;; (
  rpar S-0       ;; )
  und  S--       ;; _
  pls  S-=       ;; +
  col  S-/       ;; colon, since the physical slash key prints a semicolon

  ;; Brackets live on unusual keys on ABNT2: the physical ] key types [ and
  ;; the physical backslash key types ].
  lbkt ]         ;; [
  rbkt bksl      ;; ]
  lbrc S-]       ;; {
  rbrc S-bksl    ;; }

  ;; These four aliases use the custom key names defined in deflocalkeys-linux,
  ;; for the keys that only exist on ABNT2.
  bsl  abntbs            ;; backslash
  pipe S-abntbs          ;; vertical bar
  sl   abntslash         ;; slash
  qst  S-abntslash       ;; question mark

  ;; The key left of 1 types an apostrophe, and a double quote when shifted.
  sq   grv       ;; apostrophe
  dq   S-grv     ;; double quote

  ;; Tilde, circumflex and backtick are dead keys on ABNT2, so they only
  ;; appear after the next key press. Each macro types the dead key followed
  ;; by a space, which produces the plain character on its own.
  til  (macro apos 10 spc)      ;; tilde
  car  (macro S-apos 10 spc)    ;; circumflex
  bt   (macro S-[ 10 spc)       ;; backtick
)

(deflayer base
  esc  @numkey @symkey
  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [
  a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    scln
  z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /
)

#|
Toggle or hold Win to get this layer, which puts a numpad on the right hand and
brackets on the left hand. On the left hand the ring finger types the opening
brackets and the index finger types the closing ones: w, s and x give [, ( and
{, while r, f and v give ], ) and }. On the right hand, u, i and o give 7, 8 and
9, j, k and l give 4, 5 and 6, and m, comma and period give 1, 2 and 3, with 0
on n and the decimal point on h. The decimal comma, which is what Brazilian
numbers use, is on g, right next to the decimal point. The remaining right hand
keys carry the operators: y types =, p types *, the key right of p types /, the
c-cedilla key types + and the physical slash key types -. The keys q, e, t, a,
c and b keep typing their letters. The d key and AltGr do nothing in this
layer, so they are mapped to XX, a no-op. Win and Esc return to the base layer.

The z key reloads this configuration file (live reload), so toggling or holding
Win and pressing z applies any edit without restarting the service. If the new
file has an error, kanata keeps running with the previous configuration and
prints the error in its log.
|#
(deflayer num
  @escback @back XX
  q    @lbkt e   @rbkt t    =    7    8    9    @ast @sl
  a    @lpar XX  @rpar ,    .    4    5    6    @pls
  lrld @lbrc c   @rbrc b    0    1    2    3    -
)

#|
Toggle or hold AltGr to get this layer, which keeps programming symbols on the
left hand and on the outer keys of the right hand. On the left hand, the top
row types the shifted digits ! @ # $ and %, the home row types { ( ) } followed
by a backtick on g, and the bottom row types a backslash, [, ], * and +. On the
right hand, y, u, o and p type ^, &, a vertical bar and ?, the home row keys h,
j and l and the c-cedilla key type -, =, _ and /, and n, m, period and the
physical slash key type ~, an apostrophe, a colon and a double quote.

The characters < > and ; are left out of this layer because they are already
easy to reach on the base layer, with Shift and comma, Shift and period, and
the semicolon key itself. The i, k and comma keys, the key right of P and the
Win key do nothing in this layer, so they are mapped to XX, a no-op. AltGr and
Esc return to the base layer.
|#
(deflayer sym
  @escback XX @back
  @ex  @at  @hsh @dol @pct @car @amp XX   @pipe @qst XX
  @lbrc @lpar @rpar @rbrc @bt -    =    XX   @und @sl
  @bsl @lbkt @rbkt @ast @pls @til @sq  XX   @col @dq
)
